import std

| Tutorial section 5 brain: harvester, upgrader, builder, defender,
| and tower. The shell reads this file's exports (spawnQueue,
| behaviors, the machines, extension and tower placement policy) and
| executes them; every decision lives here. The tower shares the
| Machine/StateConfig/Transition shape with its own event vocabulary,
| and the shell drives it in a separate loop.

union CreepRole
  harvester
  upgrader
  builder
  defender

| BodyPart variant names are exact Screeps BODYPART_COST keys, so the
| generated string literals index the engine cost table and feed
| spawnCreep directly.

union BodyPart
  work
  carry
  move
  tough
  attack

| State namespace design. Each machine is verified over its own state
| union (generate rejects a machine whose state type has states the
| machine never reaches), so machine states are per-machine unions:
|
|   HarvesterState: harvesting, delivering, supporting
|   UpgraderState:  collecting, upgrading
|   BuilderState:   gathering, building, assisting
|   DefenderState:  patrolling, engaging
|   TowerState:     guarding, attacking, repairing
|
| CreepState is the shared namespace union over the ten creep state
| names. It serves the flat creep semantics: StateBehaviors totality
| and RoleSpec.initial. TowerState stays out of CreepState because the
| tower has its own behaviors record (TowerStateBehaviors) and the
| shell drives towers in a separate loop. In generated TS all of these
| are string-literal types, so each role union is assignable to
| CreepState.
|
| State names are disjoint across all machines, creep roles and tower
| alike, and the disjointness is load-bearing: the shell's machine
| registry uses the generated validHarvesterState / validUpgraderState
| / validBuilderState / validTowerState functions as ownership guards.
| An actor whose state fails a machine's valid*State check is foreign
| to that machine and the machine is skipped. Two machines sharing a
| state name would make ownership ambiguous, so variants stay
| unmerged: the builder owns `assisting`, so the harvester's
| full-and-nowhere-to-deliver state is named `supporting`; the tower
| owns `attacking`, so the defender fights in `engaging`.

union HarvesterState
  harvesting
  delivering
  supporting

union UpgraderState
  collecting
  upgrading

union BuilderState
  gathering
  building
  assisting

union DefenderState
  patrolling
  engaging

union TowerState
  guarding
  attacking
  repairing

union CreepState
  harvesting
  delivering
  supporting
  collecting
  upgrading
  gathering
  building
  assisting
  patrolling
  engaging

| The shell observes the world and emits exactly one CreepEvent per
| creep per tick. Each event carries all three observations at once:
| the creep's store level, whether any sink has room, and whether
| construction sites exist.
|
|   store level  — empty     creep has zero energy
|                  mid       neither empty nor full
|                  full      creep has zero free capacity
|   sink status  — SinksOpen some energy sink passes the free-energy
|                            filter
|                  SinksFull no energy sink in the room passes the
|                            free-energy filter
|   site status  — Site      construction sites exist
|                  NoSite    no construction sites exist
|
| An energy sink is the spawn, an extension, or a tower below
| refillTarget; the refill latch only changes which sink a delivery
| picks first. The same below-full filter drives delivery targeting
| and this observation, so a creep told to deliver always has a
| target.
|
| Naming contract (exact casing matters): each variant is the literal
| concatenation storeLevel + sinkStatus + siteStatus. The shell
| computes the event as the TypeScript template literal
|
|   `${store}${sinks}${sites}`
|
| with store in {"empty","mid","full"}, sinks in
| {"SinksOpen","SinksFull"}, sites in {"Site","NoSite"}. Rename a
| variant here and the shell fails to typecheck; add a fourth store
| level and every machine below fails generate's coverage check.
|
| Variant order is systematic: store level outermost (empty, mid,
| full), then sinks (Open, Full), then sites (Site, NoSite).

union CreepEvent
  emptySinksOpenSite
  emptySinksOpenNoSite
  emptySinksFullSite
  emptySinksFullNoSite
  midSinksOpenSite
  midSinksOpenNoSite
  midSinksFullSite
  midSinksFullNoSite
  fullSinksOpenSite
  fullSinksOpenNoSite
  fullSinksFullSite
  fullSinksFullNoSite

| The fixed action vocabulary the shell knows how to execute. States
| are tagged with actions (see StateBehaviors / TowerStateBehaviors);
| a new state added by a future spec mutation still maps into this
| vocabulary, so the shell needs no new wiring. idle pairs with
| TargetKind.none. attack serves both the tower and the defender.

union Action
  harvest
  transfer
  upgrade
  build
  attack
  repair
  idle

| The fixed target-selector vocabulary. The shell resolves each kind:
|   source           — closest active energy source
|   energySink       — closest sink with room for energy. While any
|                      tower holds the refill latch (energy dropped
|                      below towerContext.energyTarget and still
|                      short of refillTarget), the closest latched
|                      tower outranks every other sink
|   controller       — the room controller
|   constructionSite — first construction site
|   hostile          — closest hostile creep from the actor's pos
|   damagedStructure — closest owned-room structure with hits < hitsMax
|   none             — no target (pairs with Action.idle)

union TargetKind
  source
  energySink
  controller
  constructionSite
  hostile
  damagedStructure
  none

| A state's behavior: what to do and at what.

type Behavior
  action: Action
  target: TargetKind

| StateBehaviors is a record with one field per CreepState variant.
| The shell indexes behaviors[state], so a state added without a
| behavior fails the shell's typecheck.

type StateBehaviors
  harvesting: Behavior
  delivering: Behavior
  supporting: Behavior
  collecting: Behavior
  upgrading: Behavior
  gathering: Behavior
  building: Behavior
  assisting: Behavior
  patrolling: Behavior
  engaging: Behavior

harvestingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.harvest
    target: TargetKind.source

deliveringBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.transfer
    target: TargetKind.energySink

| supporting is the harvester's full-and-nowhere-to-deliver escape: a
| saturated sink set leaves nothing to transfer into, so the harvester
| spends the load on the controller. Same action pair as the builder's
| assisting, under its own name per the disjointness rule (see the
| namespace comment above).

supportingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.upgrade
    target: TargetKind.controller

collectingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.harvest
    target: TargetKind.source

upgradingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.upgrade
    target: TargetKind.controller

gatheringBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.harvest
    target: TargetKind.source

buildingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.build
    target: TargetKind.constructionSite

assistingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.upgrade
    target: TargetKind.controller

| Defender behaviors. patrolling stands guard where spawned (idle, no
| target); engaging attacks the closest hostile. The shell's
| ERR_NOT_IN_RANGE handling supplies the closing move, so melee needs
| no separate "approach" state.

patrollingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.idle
    target: TargetKind.none

engagingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.attack
    target: TargetKind.hostile

behaviors: StateBehaviors
  StateBehaviors:
    harvesting: harvestingBehavior
    delivering: deliveringBehavior
    supporting: supportingBehavior
    collecting: collectingBehavior
    upgrading: upgradingBehavior
    gathering: gatheringBehavior
    building: buildingBehavior
    assisting: assistingBehavior
    patrolling: patrollingBehavior
    engaging: engagingBehavior

| ============================ THREAT LEVEL ==========================
| ThreatLevel is the room-threat observation, made once per room per
| tick: hostile creeps present or absent. Three consumers are typed
| against this one generated union, so a rename here fails the shell's
| typecheck at each of them:
|
|   1. the tower event's first component (`${threat}...` below),
|   2. the defender machine's entire event type — a defender has no
|      carry parts, so threat is the only observation its decisions
|      read,
|   3. the DesiredCounts field names: the shell resolves each role's
|      desired count as desired[threatLevel].
|
| Hostile count and proximity are left out: no transition branches on
| either. Closest-first targeting already handles proximity, and an
| observation earns a place in an event only when some transition
| branches on it.

union ThreatLevel
  hostile
  calm

| Population policy. spawnQueue's order is the spawn priority: the
| shell walks it front to back and spawns the first role below its
| desired count.
|
| desired is a DesiredCounts record indexed by the observed
| ThreatLevel. The defender sits first in the queue with counts
| hostile: 1, calm: 0: when calm its desired count is 0 and the walk
| passes straight through; when hostiles are present, spawning the
| defender outranks every economy role. Every other role has the same
| count in both fields. A defender that outlives the raid is not
| replaced once the room is calm again.
|
| Capacity-tiered bodies. Part costs: work=100, carry=50, move=50,
| attack=80, tough=10.
|
|   heavyWorkerBody   = [work, work, carry, move, move]     cost 350
|   workerBody        = [work, carry, move]                 cost 200
|   heavyDefenderBody = [tough, attack, attack, move, move] cost 270
|   lightDefenderBody = [attack, move]                      cost 130
|
| 350 is exactly the current room energyCapacityAvailable (spawn 300 +
| 1 extension 50). Each bodies list is ordered richest first: the
| shell walks it front to back and spawns the first tier whose cost
| fits the afford basis (see spawnAffordBasis below).

workerBody: [BodyPart]
  [BodyPart.work, BodyPart.carry, BodyPart.move]

heavyWorkerBody: [BodyPart]
  [BodyPart.work, BodyPart.work, BodyPart.carry, BodyPart.move, BodyPart.move]

workerBodyTiers: [[BodyPart]]
  [heavyWorkerBody, workerBody]

| Defender tiers: the heavy tier fits current capacity (270 <= 350);
| the light tier (130) is affordable from the spawn's own energy
| regeneration, so a defender can still spawn while the economy is
| dead.

lightDefenderBody: [BodyPart]
  [BodyPart.attack, BodyPart.move]

heavyDefenderBody: [BodyPart]
  [BodyPart.tough, BodyPart.attack, BodyPart.attack, BodyPart.move, BodyPart.move]

defenderBodyTiers: [[BodyPart]]
  [heavyDefenderBody, lightDefenderBody]

| Afford basis: which Room energy figure a spawn attempt is priced
| against. The variant names are exact Screeps Room property names, so
| the generated string literal indexes the Room type directly
| (room[spawnAffordBasis]).
|
| The basis is energyAvailable. Waiting for capacity deadlocks
| recovery: with all harvesters dead, the extension never refills, so
| 350 never arrives and nothing ever spawns. At steady state the sinks
| are full and the rich tier still spawns.

union SpawnAffordBasis
  energyAvailable
  energyCapacityAvailable

spawnAffordBasis: SpawnAffordBasis
  SpawnAffordBasis.energyAvailable

| DesiredCounts field names match the ThreatLevel variant names; the
| shell indexes desired[threatLevel] with the observed value.

type DesiredCounts
  hostile: Integer
  calm: Integer

type RoleSpec
  role: CreepRole
  desired: DesiredCounts
  bodies: [[BodyPart]]
  initial: CreepState

harvesterDesired: DesiredCounts
  hostile: 2
  calm: 2

upgraderDesired: DesiredCounts
  hostile: 1
  calm: 1

builderDesired: DesiredCounts
  hostile: 2
  calm: 2

defenderDesired: DesiredCounts
  hostile: 1
  calm: 0

harvesterSpec: RoleSpec
  RoleSpec:
    role: CreepRole.harvester
    desired: harvesterDesired
    bodies: workerBodyTiers
    initial: CreepState.harvesting

upgraderSpec: RoleSpec
  RoleSpec:
    role: CreepRole.upgrader
    desired: upgraderDesired
    bodies: workerBodyTiers
    initial: CreepState.collecting

builderSpec: RoleSpec
  RoleSpec:
    role: CreepRole.builder
    desired: builderDesired
    bodies: workerBodyTiers
    initial: CreepState.gathering

defenderSpec: RoleSpec
  RoleSpec:
    role: CreepRole.defender
    desired: defenderDesired
    bodies: defenderBodyTiers
    initial: CreepState.patrolling

spawnQueue: [RoleSpec]
  [defenderSpec, harvesterSpec, upgraderSpec, builderSpec]

| Extension placement policy. The shell places construction sites for
| extensions until desiredExtensions exist (built or in progress),
| trying extensionOffsets in order relative to Spawn1 until one sticks
| — terrain may reject a candidate, so the list is ordered fallbacks.

desiredExtensions: Integer
  1

type SiteOffset
  dx: Integer
  dy: Integer

offsetEast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 2
    dy: 0

offsetWest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -2
    dy: 0

offsetSouth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: 2

offsetNorth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: -2

offsetSoutheast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 2
    dy: 2

offsetNorthwest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -2
    dy: -2

extensionOffsets: [SiteOffset]
  [offsetEast, offsetWest, offsetSouth, offsetNorth, offsetSoutheast, offsetNorthwest]

| Tower placement policy, same mechanism as extensions: the shell
| places STRUCTURE_TOWER construction sites at these offsets from
| Spawn1 until desiredTowers exist (built or in progress), once RCL
| allows it (towers unlock at RCL 3); server legality decides whether
| a candidate sticks, so the list is ordered fallbacks. The offsets
| avoid every extensionOffsets cell: extensions use
| (2,0),(-2,0),(0,2),(0,-2),(2,2),(-2,-2); towers take the remaining
| diagonals and the distance-3 cardinals.

desiredTowers: Integer
  1

offsetSouthwest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -2
    dy: 2

offsetNortheast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 2
    dy: -2

offsetFarSouth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: 3

offsetFarEast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 3
    dy: 0

offsetFarNorth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: -3

offsetFarWest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -3
    dy: 0

towerOffsets: [SiteOffset]
  [offsetSouthwest, offsetNortheast, offsetFarSouth, offsetFarEast, offsetFarNorth, offsetFarWest]

| Per-creep FSMs. Three creep machines share CreepEvent/CreepContext
| but each has its own state union, so generate verifies each machine
| total and reachable over exactly its own states. Every state handles
| every one of the 12 events, self-loops written out. Transitions are
| named top-level values, reused across states of the same machine
| wherever event and target coincide. Since state names are disjoint
| across machines, a name like toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite is globally
| unambiguous: the target names the machine.

| Context carries the same tier list as RoleSpec.bodies, so a
| machine's context cannot contradict the spawn policy; there is no
| single-body field anywhere.

type CreepContext
  role: CreepRole
  bodies: [[BodyPart]]

harvesterContext: CreepContext
  role: CreepRole.harvester
  bodies: workerBodyTiers

upgraderContext: CreepContext
  role: CreepRole.upgrader
  bodies: workerBodyTiers

builderContext: CreepContext
  role: CreepRole.builder
  bodies: workerBodyTiers

defenderContext: CreepContext
  role: CreepRole.defender
  bodies: defenderBodyTiers

| Harvester machine: harvest until full, deliver until empty; nowhere
| to put it -> support the controller.
|
| The supporting state is the full-and-nowhere-to-deliver escape. Two
| routes in:
|
|   - full + SinksFull anywhere: a full harvester with a saturated
|     sink set has no transfer target, so delivering would stall.
|   - mid + SinksFull while delivering or supporting: the behavior
|     says transfer but the target set is empty, so no API call
|     happens, the store never drains, and the event never changes.
|     Spending the remainder on the controller breaks the stall.
|
| mid + SinksOpen while supporting returns to delivering: sinks
| reopened, and delivery powers spawning, so it outranks
| controller-feeding for this role. Site presence never affects this
| role: every transition below is Site/NoSite-symmetric.

| — empty in any harvester state: back to the source (8 values; the
|   mid* four are the harvesting self-loops).

toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

| — open sinks with energy on board: deliver (mid* used by delivering/
|   supporting, full* used by all three states).

toDeliveringMidSinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

toDeliveringMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

| — saturated sinks with energy on board: support the controller (the
|   null-target-stall escapes documented above).

toSupportingMidSinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

toSupportingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

toSupportingFullSinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

harvestingConfig: StateConfig HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toHarvestingMidSinksOpenSite, toHarvestingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingMidSinksFullSite, toHarvestingMidSinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Harvesting"

| delivering and supporting have IDENTICAL transition tables (empty ->
| harvesting; SinksOpen with energy -> delivering; SinksFull with
| energy -> supporting): both states are "energy on board, dispose of
| it", and where to dispose depends only on the sink dimension.

deliveringConfig: StateConfig HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Delivering"

supportingConfig: StateConfig HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Supporting"

harvesterMachine: Machine HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Machine:
    name: "harvester"
    initial: HarvesterState.harvesting
    context: harvesterContext
    finals: {}
    states: {HarvesterState.harvesting: harvestingConfig, HarvesterState.delivering: deliveringConfig, HarvesterState.supporting: supportingConfig}

| Upgrader machine: collect until full, upgrade until empty. This
| role only spends energy on the controller and never builds, so
| every transition depends on the store level alone: full anywhere ->
| upgrading, empty anywhere -> collecting, mid self-loops.

| — full in any upgrader state: go upgrade (also the upgrading full
|   self-loops).

toUpgradingFullSinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingFullSinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingFullSinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingFullSinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

| — empty in any upgrader state: back to the source (also the
|   collecting empty self-loops).

toCollectingEmptySinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingEmptySinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingEmptySinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingEmptySinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

| — mid self-loops: keep doing what you're doing.

toCollectingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingMidSinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

collectingConfig: StateConfig UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toCollectingEmptySinksOpenSite, toCollectingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toCollectingMidSinksOpenSite, toCollectingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toCollectingMidSinksFullSite, toCollectingMidSinksFullNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Collecting"

upgradingConfig: StateConfig UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toCollectingEmptySinksOpenSite, toCollectingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toUpgradingMidSinksOpenSite, toUpgradingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toUpgradingMidSinksFullSite, toUpgradingMidSinksFullNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Upgrading"

upgraderMachine: Machine UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Machine:
    name: "upgrader"
    initial: UpgraderState.collecting
    context: upgraderContext
    finals: {}
    states: {UpgraderState.collecting: collectingConfig, UpgraderState.upgrading: upgradingConfig}

| Builder machine: gather until full; full -> build if sites exist,
| else assist. A builder spends energy on sites and the controller
| and never transfers, so every transition below is
| SinksOpen/SinksFull-symmetric.
|
| full*Site while already in building stays in building. Events are
| computed at tick start, before actions, so observing full here
| means the previous build spent nothing (site unreachable that tick,
| ramp-up ordering). The site is still observed, so staying on it is
| a retry against a live target; full*NoSite has its own pair of
| events routing to assisting.
|
| mid*NoSite while building -> assisting: the last site finished
| mid-drain, and with no build target left the remainder goes to the
| controller. mid*Site while assisting pulls the builder back to
| building.

| — empty in any builder state: back to the source (the mid* four are
|   the gathering self-loops).

toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

| — a site exists and there is energy on board: build (mid* used by
|   building/assisting, full* used by all three states).

toBuildingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

toBuildingMidSinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

toBuildingFullSinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

| — no site and energy on board: assist the controller.

toAssistingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

toAssistingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

gatheringConfig: StateConfig BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite, toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite, toGatheringMidSinksOpenSite, toGatheringMidSinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringMidSinksFullSite, toGatheringMidSinksFullNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite, toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksFullSite, toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Gathering"

| building and assisting have IDENTICAL transition tables (empty ->
| gathering; energy + Site -> building; energy + NoSite -> assisting):
| both states are "energy on board, spend it", and where to spend
| depends only on the site dimension.

buildingConfig: StateConfig BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite, toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksOpenSite, toAssistingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksFullSite, toAssistingMidSinksFullNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite, toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksFullSite, toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Building"

assistingConfig: StateConfig BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite, toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksOpenSite, toAssistingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksFullSite, toAssistingMidSinksFullNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite, toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksFullSite, toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Assisting"

builderMachine: Machine BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Machine:
    name: "builder"
    initial: BuilderState.gathering
    context: builderContext
    finals: {}
    states: {BuilderState.gathering: gatheringConfig, BuilderState.building: buildingConfig, BuilderState.assisting: assistingConfig}

| ============================= DEFENDER =============================
| The defender's event type is ThreatLevel: with no carry parts it
| has no store to observe, so the room threat is the only input its
| decisions read. The shell's machine registry pairs each machine
| with its own emitter, and the defender's emitter reports the same
| room-threat observation the tower and the spawn walk consume.
|
| Both states carry the identical two transitions, so the target
| depends only on the event: hostile -> engaging (attack the closest
| hostile; the shell's ERR_NOT_IN_RANGE handling closes distance),
| calm -> patrolling (idle at post). Every state covers both events
| and there are no finals; generate verifies this.

toEngagingHostile: Transition DefenderState ThreatLevel CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: ThreatLevel.hostile
    target: DefenderState.engaging
    guard: ()

toPatrollingCalm: Transition DefenderState ThreatLevel CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: ThreatLevel.calm
    target: DefenderState.patrolling
    guard: ()

patrollingConfig: StateConfig DefenderState ThreatLevel CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toEngagingHostile, toPatrollingCalm]
    label: "Patrolling"

engagingConfig: StateConfig DefenderState ThreatLevel CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toEngagingHostile, toPatrollingCalm]
    label: "Engaging"

defenderMachine: Machine DefenderState ThreatLevel CreepContext
  Machine:
    name: "defender"
    initial: DefenderState.patrolling
    context: defenderContext
    finals: {}
    states: {DefenderState.patrolling: patrollingConfig, DefenderState.engaging: engagingConfig}

| ============================== TOWER ===============================
| Same Machine/StateConfig/Transition shape as the creep machines,
| with its own event and context types. The shell drives towers in a
| separate loop (one TowerEvent per tower per tick), with
| validTowerState as the ownership guard, exactly like the creep
| registry.

| Each TowerEvent carries three observations at once, same
| construction and same template-literal contract as CreepEvent:
|
|   threat    — hostile     hostile creeps present in the room
|               calm        none
|   integrity — Damage      some owned-room structure has hits < hitsMax
|               Intact      none
|   reserve   — ReserveOk   tower energy >= attackReserve
|               ReserveLow  tower energy below the attack reserve
|
| The shell computes the event as `${threat}${integrity}${reserve}`,
| where the threat component is the same ThreatLevel value that
| drives the defender machine and the DesiredCounts index. Rename a
| variant here and the shell fails to typecheck.
|
| Tower energy enters the event as a single bit, above or below
| attackReserve, because one decision branches on it: repair. Repair
| spends energy with no floor, so repairing while low would leave the
| next raider facing an empty tower; a ReserveLow tower waits for
| refill before repairing. Attack ignores reserve — a drained tower's
| attack is a harmless no-op, so shooting is always safe. Refill is
| delivery-driven and needs no tower decision. attackReserve <
| energyTarget matters: a ReserveLow tower is already below the latch
| trigger, so it holds the delivery priority (see the refill decision
| below) and the wait ends with the refill campaign.

union TowerEvent
  hostileDamageReserveOk
  hostileDamageReserveLow
  hostileIntactReserveOk
  hostileIntactReserveLow
  calmDamageReserveOk
  calmDamageReserveLow
  calmIntactReserveOk
  calmIntactReserveLow

| The refill decision answers two separate questions:
|
|   Is the tower a delivery target?  Yes whenever energy <
|   refillTarget (full capacity, 1000) — the same "has room?" rule
|   every spawn and extension answers. A tower at 990 is an ordinary
|   sink and gets topped back to full by the ordinary delivery
|   rotation. If the latch gated membership instead, a full tower
|   that fires one shot would sit at 990 forever: below full, above
|   the reopen line, and never fed again.
|
|   How urgently?  A hysteresis latch: it opens the moment energy
|   drops below energyTarget (500) and closes only when delivery
|   brings the tower back to refillTarget. While open, refilling
|   that tower is the delivery priority — the energySink selector
|   answers with the closest latched tower before any spawn or
|   extension. A plain threshold here would flap: one shot fired at
|   501 demotes, one delivery promotes. The latch opens only on a
|   real 500-point drain and holds the front of the line until full,
|   one refill campaign per drain.
|
| The shell holds the latch bit per tower id (Memory.towerRefill).
| The below-full filter drives both delivery targeting and the
| SinksFull observation, so the observed fact always matches the
| available action.
|
| TowerContext carries desired alongside energyTarget because Paradox
| rejects single-field product types (error 23). desired references
| desiredTowers, so the context cannot contradict the placement
| policy — same pattern as CreepContext.bodies referencing
| workerBodyTiers.

type TowerContext
  energyTarget: Integer
  refillTarget: Integer
  desired: Integer
  attackReserve: Integer

towerEnergyTarget: Integer
  500

| refillTarget: where a refill campaign ends — full capacity (the
| engine's TOWER_CAPACITY, 1000). Once the latch opens at
| energyTarget, creeps keep the tower first in line until it reads
| this. Must match dox/invader RaidPolicy.towerFullEnergy, the raid
| mod's fair-fight bar: a room is only raid-eligible while a full
| tower stands.

towerRefillTarget: Integer
  1000

| attackReserve: the repair floor. Calm-time repair runs only while
| energy >= this; below it the tower guards and waits for delivery.
| 200 = 20 shots held back for the next raid. Must stay below
| towerEnergyTarget: a tower under the latch trigger holds the
| delivery priority, so the wait for refill always ends.

towerAttackReserve: Integer
  200

towerContext: TowerContext
  energyTarget: towerEnergyTarget
  refillTarget: towerRefillTarget
  desired: desiredTowers
  attackReserve: towerAttackReserve

| Tower policy: attack comes first, and repair only runs with a
| reserve behind it.
|
|   hostile**                -> attacking  (reserve ignored: shoot
|                                           with whatever is in the
|                                           tank)
|   calmDamageReserveOk      -> repairing  (heal to hitsMax)
|   calmDamageReserveLow     -> guarding   (hold the reserve; wait
|                                           for delivery refill)
|   calmIntact*              -> guarding
|
| All three states carry the identical eight transitions, so the
| target depends only on the event. The uniform machine shape keeps
| the shell a generic interpreter: one registry, one transition call,
| no special-casing the tower. The current state still matters
| downstream: towerBehaviors[state] picks the action each tick.

toAttackingHostileDamageReserveOk: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.hostileDamageReserveOk
    target: TowerState.attacking
    guard: ()

toAttackingHostileDamageReserveLow: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.hostileDamageReserveLow
    target: TowerState.attacking
    guard: ()

toAttackingHostileIntactReserveOk: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.hostileIntactReserveOk
    target: TowerState.attacking
    guard: ()

toAttackingHostileIntactReserveLow: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.hostileIntactReserveLow
    target: TowerState.attacking
    guard: ()

toRepairingCalmDamageReserveOk: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.calmDamageReserveOk
    target: TowerState.repairing
    guard: ()

toGuardingCalmDamageReserveLow: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.calmDamageReserveLow
    target: TowerState.guarding
    guard: ()

toGuardingCalmIntactReserveOk: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.calmIntactReserveOk
    target: TowerState.guarding
    guard: ()

toGuardingCalmIntactReserveLow: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.calmIntactReserveLow
    target: TowerState.guarding
    guard: ()

guardingConfig: StateConfig TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toAttackingHostileDamageReserveOk, toAttackingHostileDamageReserveLow, toAttackingHostileIntactReserveOk, toAttackingHostileIntactReserveLow, toRepairingCalmDamageReserveOk, toGuardingCalmDamageReserveLow, toGuardingCalmIntactReserveOk, toGuardingCalmIntactReserveLow]
    label: "Guarding"

attackingConfig: StateConfig TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toAttackingHostileDamageReserveOk, toAttackingHostileDamageReserveLow, toAttackingHostileIntactReserveOk, toAttackingHostileIntactReserveLow, toRepairingCalmDamageReserveOk, toGuardingCalmDamageReserveLow, toGuardingCalmIntactReserveOk, toGuardingCalmIntactReserveLow]
    label: "Attacking"

repairingConfig: StateConfig TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toAttackingHostileDamageReserveOk, toAttackingHostileDamageReserveLow, toAttackingHostileIntactReserveOk, toAttackingHostileIntactReserveLow, toRepairingCalmDamageReserveOk, toGuardingCalmDamageReserveLow, toGuardingCalmIntactReserveOk, toGuardingCalmIntactReserveLow]
    label: "Repairing"

| Reachability from guarding: hostile** -> attacking,
| calmDamageReserveOk -> repairing — all three states reachable, every
| state covers all eight events, no finals, no deadlock. generate
| verifies exactly this.

towerMachine: Machine TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Machine:
    name: "tower"
    initial: TowerState.guarding
    context: towerContext
    finals: {}
    states: {TowerState.guarding: guardingConfig, TowerState.attacking: attackingConfig, TowerState.repairing: repairingConfig}

| TowerStateBehaviors is a record with one field per TowerState
| variant. The shell indexes towerBehaviors[state], so a tower state
| added without a behavior fails the shell's typecheck.

type TowerStateBehaviors
  guarding: Behavior
  attacking: Behavior
  repairing: Behavior

guardingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.idle
    target: TargetKind.none

attackingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.attack
    target: TargetKind.hostile

repairingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.repair
    target: TargetKind.damagedStructure

towerBehaviors: TowerStateBehaviors
  TowerStateBehaviors:
    guarding: guardingBehavior
    attacking: attackingBehavior
    repairing: repairingBehavior
